DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING - 

1. Climbing Stairs

QUESTION - https://leetcode.com/problems/climbing-stairs/?envType=problem-list-v2&envId=dynamic-programming 
there are mainly two types of DP's - top down and bottom up. in top-down you try to think from final destination or solution, like you're climbing stairs upto the final point, in top down you think n-1, n-2, n-3,.. and so on, usually it requires recursive thinking including caching the values. in bottom-up you are building the solution from base upto n, like if you're trying to reach to the top of the staircase then, you start from the ground and try to reach to n.
usually bottom up solution is preferred. 
def climbingStairs(n):
    if(n<=1):
        return 1
    dp = [0]*(n+1)
    dp[0], dp[1] = 1, 1 # there is only one way to reach to the beginning and to the first staircase
    for i in range(2, n+1):
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2]
        
    return dp[n]
    
in this type of question it imitates Fibonacci question. also worth noting that this question is not for greedy, why because in this each step is determined but in greedy each step is determined by the value of each element at that step. idk if it makes sense, it made sense to me. 
here we are making a dp array to store for each staircase how many steps it took and it is basic knowledge that the amount of steps taken to reach any step is the sum of the amount of steps taken to reach the previous as well as previous to previous step because either one or two steps is allowed. so similarly if three steps were also involved we would've added dp[i-3] as well.

